WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:04.037
The town of Soda Springs, Idaho,
in the USA, lives up to its name.

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:07.173
Because here in Geyser Park,
in the center of town,

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.543
there is a geyser that shoots
soda water up into the sky.

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:13.146
As in: carbonated water,
seltzer, sparkling water,

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:14.581
whatever you want to call it.

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:16.316
The stuff you'd find in a bottle.

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:18.284
The geyser here isn't boiling,
that's not steam.

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:22.122
Underneath the town, there's a natural
reservoir of carbonated mineral water.

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:26.326
But while the water is natural,
the geyser is manmade and accidental.

00:00:26.326 --> 00:00:34.067
- 1937, a group of local businessmen
started talking about a pool for Soda Springs,

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:36.870
and they started drilling.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.774
They were about out of money
and about out of eight-inch pipe.

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:42.842
They were down 315 feet.

00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:44.177
And they quit for the evening.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:48.681
They were having dinner,
and they heard this loud noise.

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:53.520
They saw this column of water shooting
into the air and a crowd gathering.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.723
The businesses downtown were
getting kind of anxious

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:58.258
because water was going everywhere.

00:00:58.258 --> 00:00:59.559
It was in the middle of December.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:01.461
And then they had to figure out
how to cap it.

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:03.196
It took them about two weeks.

00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:06.132
When they stopped drilling,
they were right next to the water,

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:08.802
but they were in a pocket of CO₂ gas and

00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:14.007
as they stopped, they combined, and that
formed the pressure that released it.

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:17.210
The CO₂ gas also cooled the water

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:21.881
to where it was not hot enough
for a swimming pool.

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:24.050
- Turns out natural carbonated
water is a thing that happens

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.218
in a few places around the world

00:01:25.218 --> 00:01:29.022
where there's underground
water and carbon dioxide held at pressure.

00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:33.359
The geyser wasn't a complete surprise.
...I mean, in the moment, it probably was,

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:37.163
but the town had already been called
Soda Springs for decades,

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:41.267
because there were natural carbonated
springs near here that you could bathe in.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:44.237
The first English-language accounts
date from the days of the Oregon Trail,

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:45.472
which passed through this area,

00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:49.142
although the springs were used well before
European settlers ever got to the area.

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:53.313
- The pioneers would pick the warm
springs out to do laundry,

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:58.651
and they would mix their flour
to make their bread with the soda springs.

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:01.187
Because they didn't need yeast
and their bread would rise.

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:04.958
And of the hundreds of springs
that were here when the early pioneers

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:10.697
came through, there are five
that I know of left that have soda water.

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:13.933
The majority of those
springs were covered up in

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:17.604
1923 when they built the Alexander Dam.

00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:18.805
The rest of them...

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:21.341
spring was in the way,
it got filled in and covered over.

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:23.943
At one time, they bottled the water.

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:28.281
Not from any of the springs the city has,
but they bottled it and sold it worldwide.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:32.619
- So when this thing started, it wasn't
so much a geyser as it was a blown-out well,

00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:35.355
putting out enough water to flood
parts of the town with seltzer.

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:38.791
The entire underground reservoir
was pushing up through the borehole.

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:43.296
- The geyser ran continuously for a while
and the officials at the Park

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:47.100
must have said something
about some irregularities in Old Faithful.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.971
And the Secretary of the Interior sent
a telegram to the city offices telling

00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:56.643
them to shut the geyser off immediately
and notify them they had done that.

00:02:56.643 --> 00:03:01.948
So they did. And then it was determined
that had no impact on the geyser basin.

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:06.419
And so they gave them back
the permission to operate the geyser.

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:11.291
When I was a boy in the 1960s,

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:16.329
a city employee would go into a little
shed where the control for the valve was

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:20.633
and manually turn the valve on
and manually turn it off.

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:24.437
If the wind was blowing towards town,
he was not supposed to turn it on.

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:28.675
Now they have a electronic timer
and it goes off every hour on the hour.

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:33.213
- Someone realized that if they put
a timer valve on their new geyser, well,

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:35.081
that could be a pretty good
tourist attraction.

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:38.518
And the result
is this colossal 20 meter eruption,

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:41.888
for eight minutes,
on the hour, every hour.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:44.190
All the orange stuff around
it is travertine, mineral deposits

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.660
that have built up over the years,
because this is mineral water.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.197
- You don't want to park your car
where it might get overspray

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:54.300
from the geyser,
because of the mineral in the water.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.438
If you did that over time, you'd have a
real problem getting that hard water off.

00:03:58.438 --> 00:04:01.741
Some of the graves on the northeast
corner of the cemetery

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:06.145
have been impacted by the overspray,
as has the walkway around it.

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:08.448
It's all coated in a rock.

00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:11.718
The headstones,
some of them are not readable anymore.

00:04:11.718 --> 00:04:15.955
- The town's publicity says that
this is the world's only captive geyser.

00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:17.824
That does depend on how you define it.

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:20.493
There's a couple of places in Germany
that would probably take issue with that.

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:21.995
It does seem to be
the only one in America.

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:25.465
And as far as I can tell, it's
the only one on a regular timer,

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:29.269
where the public can just turn up
and watch for no admission fee.

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:33.039
And I know the obvious question is,
what does that water taste like?

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:35.875
I could try and stand over there
with a glass for the next eruption,

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:38.845
catch the water, get soaked,
but I don't need to.

00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:41.748
Because at Octagon Springs
on the other side of the railway tracks,

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:45.518
the city has thoughtfully provided a
little ramp, and you can just get a glass.

00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:53.126
That's really nice.

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:55.528
I don't know what I was expecting.

00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:58.498
That's just nice, lightly
carbonated water. It's not too fizzy.

00:04:58.498 --> 00:04:59.032
That's...

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:02.635
I would happily buy that in a bottle.

